'''''Margall v. Sant Cristòfor Municipal Credit Union''''' (1978) was a landmark {{w|court case}} in [[Balboa]] in which [[Teresa Margall i Pons]], founder of the Balboan Women's Civic Association, challenged the Sant Cristòfor Municipal Credit Union's policy of requiring a husband's co-signature for any {{w|loan}} issued to a married woman. The court ruled against Margall, holding that the [[Twelve Guarantees]] did not cover {{w|sex discrimination|sex-based discrimination}}. The ruling confirmed that women had no constitutional recourse against sex-based discrimination and became a rallying point for the campaign that led to the [[Constitutional Amendment on Sex-Based Discrimination]] in 1986.

== Background ==

Under residual Volisanian {{w|civil law (common law)|civil law}} provisions that had not been repealed by the 1974 constitution, married women in Balboa were subject to {{w|coverture|coverture-style}} restrictions on their legal capacity. Married women could not independently own {{w|property}} or enter {{w|contract|contracts}} without their husband's consent. The Sant Cristòfor Municipal Credit Union, a public lending institution, applied this principle in its lending policy: any {{w|loan}} application from a married woman required the co-signature of her husband, on the grounds that married women's property legally belonged to their husbands.

[[Teresa Margall i Pons]], a married woman, was the founder of the Balboan Women's Civic Association, an advocacy group established in 1968 during the [[International Mandate of Sant Cristòfor|Sant Cristòfor mandate]] period. In 1978, Margall applied for a business loan to fund the association's expansion. The Credit Union denied the application on the grounds that her husband had not co-signed the application. Margall's husband had no involvement in the association and had not consented to the loan.

== Case ==

Margall brought a case before the Sant Cristòfor Civil Court, arguing that the Credit Union's policy violated the [[Twelve Guarantees]]' general {{w|anti-discrimination}} provisions. The Guarantees, entrenched in the Balboan constitution, prohibited discrimination on the basis of race, ethnicity, religion, and national origin. Margall's legal team argued that the Guarantees' general language should be interpreted to encompass sex-based discrimination, or alternatively that the policy constituted {{w|unconstitutional discrimination}} under the constitution's equal protection provisions.

The Credit Union defended its policy on the grounds that the lending requirement was derived from established civil law principles regarding married women's property rights and that the Twelve Guarantees' enumerated categories were exhaustive. The Credit Union argued that the court could not extend the Guarantees' scope to include sex without a constitutional amendment.

== Ruling ==

The court ruled in favour of the Credit Union. The ruling held that the Twelve Guarantees' enumerated categories — race, ethnicity, religion, and national origin — were exhaustive and that sex-based discrimination was not covered. The court further held that the Credit Union's lending policy was consistent with the residual civil law provisions governing married women's property rights, which had not been repealed by the 1974 constitution.

The ruling confirmed what women's organisations had long argued: that without an explicit {{w|constitutional amendment}} adding sex to the Guarantees' protected categories, women had no legal recourse against {{w|sex discrimination}} in Balboa.

== Significance ==

The Margall case became a rallying point for the women's movement in Balboa. Margall's public profile following the ruling made her one of the most recognisable women's rights advocates in the country. The case was cited extensively in the campaign for the [[Constitutional Amendment on Sex-Based Discrimination]], which was passed in 1986 and added sex to the protected categories under the Twelve Guarantees.

The case is widely regarded as the catalyst for the organised campaign for constitutional reform, as it demonstrated in concrete terms that the existing legal framework provided no protection against sex-based discrimination. The ruling was later overturned ''de facto'' by the 1986 amendment, which prohibited the type of discrimination that Margall had experienced.

